---
import BaseLayout from '~/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Figure from '~/components/Figure.astro';
import Assumptions from '~/components/Assumptions.astro';
import Callout from '~/components/Callout.astro';
import Math from '~/components/Math.astro';
import Badge from '~/components/Badge.astro';
import Cite from '~/components/Cite.astro';
import SpookyBasis from '~/islands/SpookyBasis';
import SpookyTwist from '~/islands/SpookyTwist';
import SpookyFragments from '~/islands/SpookyFragments';
import SpookyAttention from '~/islands/SpookyAttention';
import { BOHR, MAGIC_THETA, RC_PAPER } from '~/lib/models/spookynet';

const toy = 'A SpookyNet with the paper’s functional form (Eqs. 1, 9–12, 18–21, 24) and fixed random weights: F = 12 features, K = 8 radial functions, three modules, one residual block per resmlp, silu at its initial α = 1, β = 1.702, r_cut = 10 a₀. Not trained; no trained SpookyNet weights have been released.';

const basisAssumptions = [
  { term: 'Basis', value: 'ρ_k(r) = C(K−1, k) x^k (1 − x)^{K−1−k} f_cut(r), x = e^{−γr} (Eqs. 14–16). γ is learned in SpookyNet; the slider starts at its initial value ½ a₀⁻¹ = 0.94 Å⁻¹.' },
  { term: 'Units', value: `Distances in Å; the paper works in bohr (1 a₀ = ${BOHR.toFixed(6)} Å), so r_cut = 10 a₀ = ${RC_PAPER.toFixed(3)} Å.` },
  { term: 'Live check', value: 'Σ_k ρ_k(r) is compared with f_cut(r) on the plotted grid.' },
];
const twistAssumptions = [
  { term: 'Geometry', value: `Fe at the origin, six O at 2.0 Å: three at polar angle θ and azimuths τ, τ + 120°, τ + 240°, three at 180° − θ and azimuths 0°, 120°, 240°. τ = 60° with θ = ${MAGIC_THETA.toFixed(2)}° is a regular octahedron, τ = 0° a trigonal prism. A rigid geometric path; no hydrogens, no energies are compared with any reference.` },
  { term: 'Live', value: 'a_l = Σ_m (Σ_j Y_l^m(r̂_j))² for the metal with the paper’s unnormalised real harmonics (Eq. 17); the toy model’s feature vector x after modules 1 and 2, compared with its value at τ = 60°.' },
  { term: 'Toy model', value: toy },
];
const fragAssumptions = [
  { term: 'Systems', value: 'LiF, NaCl, HF along x (d = bond length); benzene (C–C 1.397 Å, C–H 1.084 Å) and water (O–H 0.9572 Å, 104.52°) on the ring axis with one O–H pointing at the ring centre (d = ring centre to O); two benzenes face to face (d = ring to ring). Rigid fragments.' },
  { term: 'Energies', value: 'Network energy Σ_i E_i only (Eq. 21), in toy units; E_rep, E_ele and E_vdw are not added. Integer limits: the same model on each fragment alone, with all of Q on one fragment and none on the other.' },
  { term: 'Toy model', value: toy },
];
const attnAssumptions = [
  { term: 'Inputs', value: 'Queries, keys and values of the toy model’s second nonlocal block for C₁₀H₂ (Q = 0) and C₁₀H₃⁺ (Q = +1) on the idealised chain of the long-range page (C–C 1.28 Å, C–H 1.06 Å, CH₂ end 1.09 Å at ±60°).' },
  { term: 'Estimator', value: 'Positive (FAVOR+): φ(x) = exp(ω·x′ − |x′|²/2)/√m, x′ = x/F^{1/4}; sin/cos: exp(|x′|²/2)/√m [cos ω·x′, sin ω·x′]. ω drawn in orthogonal blocks with rows rescaled to χ-distributed norms, as in the reference code. The reference code also adds small constants for numerical stability, omitted here.' },
  { term: 'Sharpening', value: 'The slider multiplies queries and keys by a common factor to make attention more peaked. It is a didactic knob, not part of SpookyNet.' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="SpookyNet"
  description="SpookyNet: a message-passing potential that takes total charge and spin as inputs, uses s-, p- and d-like interactions, and couples all atoms through linear-cost self-attention. What one update can see, where a total charge goes, what separated fragments see, and how FAVOR+ approximates attention, computed live."
>
  <article class="page-grid model-page">
    <header class="wide head">
      <p class="eyebrow"><a href="/models/">Models</a> · Model family</p>
      <h1 class="h-display">SpookyNet</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Atomic numbers and positions do not say how many electrons a molecule has or how they are paired. SpookyNet adds both as inputs, the
        total charge Q and the number of unpaired electrons S, and adds a second channel of communication between atoms that ignores distance
        altogether: self-attention over every atom in the structure <Cite id="unke2021spookynet" locator="Introduction; Eqs. (1)–(2)" />. The name
        refers to Einstein’s “spooky action at a distance”. This page takes the architecture apart with live calculations: what one update can
        see, where a total charge goes, and what two molecules far apart still know about each other.
      </p>
      <dl class="glance">
        <div><dt>Energy</dt><dd>Σ E<sub>i</sub> + E<sub>rep</sub> (ZBL-based) + E<sub>ele</sub> (charges) + E<sub>vdw</sub> (D4)</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Inputs</dt><dd>Positions, elements, total charge Q and number of unpaired electrons S</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Local update</dt><dd>s-, p- and d-like functions (l ≤ 2) inside r<sub>cut</sub> = 10 a₀, reduced to invariants</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Nonlocal update</dt><dd>Self-attention over all atoms, FAVOR+ (linear in the number of atoms)</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Paper’s models</dt><dd>T = 6 modules, F = 128 features, trained separately for each dataset</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Introduced</dt><dd>Nat. Commun. 2021</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Code</dt><dd><a href="https://github.com/OUnke/SpookyNet">OUnke/SpookyNet</a> (MIT); no trained weights released</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <p class="rule-note"><Badge label="schematic" /> The text is about the <strong>architecture</strong>. No trained checkpoint exists to run, so Figs. 2–4 use a <strong>toy model</strong> with the same functional form and random weights, and show only what holds for any weights.</p>
    </header>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="energy-h">
      <h2 id="energy-h" class="h-section">Inputs, embeddings and the energy</h2>
      <p>
        Each atom starts from the sum of three embeddings: one for its element and one each for the charge and spin of the whole structure
        <Cite id="unke2021spookynet" locator="Eq. (1)" />. A chain of T interaction modules then refines the features, each adding a local and a
        nonlocal update <Cite id="unke2021spookynet" locator="Eq. (2)" />:
      </p>
      <Math display number="1" tex={String.raw`\mathbf{x}^{(0)}_i = \mathbf{e}_{Z_i} + \mathbf{e}_{Q,i} + \mathbf{e}_{S,i}, \qquad \mathbf{x}_i^{(t)} = \mathbf{x}_i^{(t-1)} + \mathrm{local}^{(t)}\big(\{\mathbf{x}_j^{(t-1)}, \vec r_{ij}\}_{j\in\mathcal N(i)}\big) + \mathrm{nonlocal}^{(t)}\big(\{\mathbf{x}^{(t-1)}\}\big)`} />
      <p>
        Every module also emits an output y<sup>(t)</sup>; their sum is the atomic descriptor f<sub>i</sub>, from which a linear readout gives the atomic
        energy E<sub>i</sub> and a partial charge <Cite id="unke2021spookynet" locator="Eqs. (3), (21), (24)" />. The total energy adds three
        physically motivated terms <Cite id="unke2021spookynet" locator="Eq. (4)" />:
      </p>
      <Math display number="2" tex={String.raw`E_{\mathrm{pot}} = \sum_{i=1}^{N} E_i + E_{\mathrm{rep}} + E_{\mathrm{ele}} + E_{\mathrm{vdw}}`} />
      <p>
        E<sub>rep</sub> is a screened nuclear repulsion inside the cutoff based on the Ziegler–Biersack–Littmark potential
        <Cite id="ziegler1985zbl" />, with its parameters starting from the literature values and then trained
        <Cite id="unke2021spookynet" locator="Methods, Eq. (22); Training and hyperparameters" />. E<sub>ele</sub> is the Coulomb energy of the
        predicted charges, softened to 1/√(r² + 1) (in bohr) at short range by a smooth switch between r<sub>cut</sub>/4 and 3r<sub>cut</sub>/4
        <Cite id="unke2021spookynet" locator="Methods, Eqs. (23), (25)" />. E<sub>vdw</sub> is the two-body part of D4 dispersion
        <Cite id="caldeweyher2019d4" />, evaluated with SpookyNet’s own charges, with the functional-dependent D4 parameters learned
        <Cite id="unke2021spookynet" locator="Methods, Eq. (27)" />. The authors present these terms as fixing the behaviour at very short and very
        long distances so the network has a smaller range of energies to learn <Cite id="unke2021spookynet" locator="Fig. 1c" />; they follow
        PhysNet and earlier work <Cite id="unke2019physnet" />.
      </p>
      <p>
        The element embedding is e<sub>Z</sub> = M d<sub>Z</sub> + ẽ<sub>Z</sub>, where d<sub>Z</sub> is a fixed 20-entry vector of the element’s
        ground-state electron configuration (occupations of 1s to 6p, valence s, p, d and f counts, and Z, each divided by its value for radon) and
        ẽ<sub>Z</sub> a free vector per element; the authors describe M d<sub>Z</sub> as a bias towards representations in which similar elements
        are similar <Cite id="unke2021spookynet" locator="Methods, Eq. (9); Supplementary Information, element descriptor table" />.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="local-h">
      <h2 id="local-h" class="h-section">Local interactions shaped like s, p and d orbitals</h2>
      <p>
        Inside the cutoff, atom i collects from its neighbours three kinds of sums: radial (s), vector (p, l = 1) and quadrupolar (d, l = 2), each
        a learned per-feature mix of K basis functions <Cite id="unke2021spookynet" locator="Methods, Eqs. (12)–(13)" />. The p and d sums are
        made invariant by scalar products of two learned projections:
      </p>
      <Math display number="3" tex={String.raw`\mathbf{s}_i = \sum_{j\in\mathcal N(i)} \mathrm{resmlp}_\mathrm{s}(\tilde{\mathbf{x}}_j)\odot \mathbf{G}_\mathrm{s}\mathbf{g}_\mathrm{s}(\vec r_{ij}),\quad \vec{\mathbf{p}}_i = \sum_{j} \mathrm{resmlp}_\mathrm{p}(\tilde{\mathbf{x}}_j)\odot \mathbf{G}_\mathrm{p}\vec{\mathbf{g}}_\mathrm{p}(\vec r_{ij}),\quad \mathbf{l}_i = \mathrm{resmlp}_\mathrm{l}\big(\mathbf{c}_i + \mathbf{s}_i + \langle \mathbf{P}_1\vec{\mathbf{p}}_i, \mathbf{P}_2\vec{\mathbf{p}}_i\rangle + \langle \mathbf{D}_1\vec{\mathbf{d}}_i, \mathbf{D}_2\vec{\mathbf{d}}_i\rangle\big)`} />
      <p>
        (d<sub>i</sub> is formed like p<sub>i</sub> with l = 2.) Each basis function is a radial part times a real spherical harmonic,
        <sub>k</sub>g<sub>l</sub><sup>m</sup> = ρ<sub>k</sub>(r) Y<sub>l</sub><sup>m</sup>(r̂), and the radial part is a Bernstein polynomial of
        e<sup>−γr</sup> <Cite id="unke2021spookynet" locator="Methods, Eqs. (13)–(16)" />:
      </p>
      <Math display number="4" tex={String.raw`\rho_k(r) = \binom{K-1}{k}\, x^k (1-x)^{K-1-k}\, f_{\mathrm{cut}}(r),\qquad x = e^{-\gamma r},\qquad f_{\mathrm{cut}}(r) = \exp\!\Big(-\frac{r^2}{(r_{\mathrm{cut}}-r)(r_{\mathrm{cut}}+r)}\Big)\ (r<r_{\mathrm{cut}})`} />
      <p>
        The exponential maps [0, ∞) onto (0, 1], where Bernstein polynomials can approximate any continuous function; the authors point out that
        learned functions can then vary less quickly at large r, like orbitals that decay exponentially <Cite id="unke2021spookynet" locator="Methods, after Eq. (16)" />.
        The harmonics of Eq. (17) are the standard real ones without the factor √(4π/(2l + 1)), so Σ<sub>m</sub> Y<sub>l</sub><sup>m</sup>(û)Y<sub>l</sub><sup>m</sup>(v̂) = P<sub>l</sub>(û·v̂)
        (checked in the test suite). Including d-like terms costs one sum per neighbour, not one per pair of neighbours: angles enter without
        computing any angle <Cite id="unke2021spookynet" locator="Introduction" />.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="wide fig-wrap">
      <Figure title="The radial basis" labels={['live-calculation']} number={1} id="fig-basis" framed={false}>
        <SpookyBasis client:visible />
        <Fragment slot="caption">
          K exponential Bernstein functions under the cutoff. Their sum is the cutoff function itself, and their peaks crowd towards the nucleus:
          equal steps in k are steps of ln((K − 1)/k)/γ in r.
        </Fragment>
        <Assumptions slot="assumptions" items={basisAssumptions} />
        <Fragment slot="source">Eqs. (14)–(16) of <Cite id="unke2021spookynet" locator="Methods" />. Computed by <code>src/lib/models/spookynet.ts</code> (partition of unity tested).</Fragment>
      </Figure>
    </div>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="twist-h">
      <h2 id="twist-h" class="h-section">What one update can and cannot see</h2>
      <p>
        With all neighbours at one distance, the radial factors are common to all of them, and what an update can tell apart reduces to angular
        power-spectrum invariants a<sub>l</sub> = Σ<sub>m</sub>(Σ<sub>j</sub> Y<sub>l</sub><sup>m</sup>(r̂<sub>j</sub>))². The authors note that a
        square planar and a tetrahedral environment share their s and p invariants and that SpookyNet’s update, with L = 2, separates them; with one
        step it told apart two of their five hard environment pairs, with two or more steps all five, and adding f- or g-like terms gave little
        gain for the cost <Cite id="unke2021spookynet" locator="Supplementary Information, completeness of atomic descriptors" />. Figure 2 builds
        a pair that L = 2 cannot separate from a familiar shape change.
      </p>
      <Callout kind="derivation" title="A twist the first module cannot see">
        <p id="twist">
          In module 1, x̃<sub>j</sub> depends only on the element of j and on the composition, Q and S of the whole structure (Eq. 1), so for a centre
          with six identical ligands at one distance every neighbour contributes the same vectors and the same ρ<sub>k</sub>. The centre’s s, p and d
          sums are then fixed multiples of A<sub>l</sub><sup>m</sup> = Σ<sub>j</sub> Y<sub>l</sub><sup>m</sup>(r̂<sub>j</sub>) for l ≤ 2, and so are its
          invariants; the nonlocal block of module 1 sees only elements too. Put three ligands at polar angle θ and azimuths τ + 2πk/3 and three at
          π − θ and 2πk/3. For m = ±1 and ±2, Y<sub>l</sub><sup>m</sup> carries cos mφ or sin mφ, and Σ<sub>k</sub> e<sup>im(τ + 2πk/3)</sup> = 0 over
          each triangle. For m = 0, Y<sub>1</sub><sup>0</sup> = z cancels between the triangles and Y<sub>2</sub><sup>0</sup> = P<sub>2</sub>(cos θ) gives
          6P<sub>2</sub>(cos θ) for any τ. So every l ≤ 2 sum is independent of the twist τ, at any θ, and the centre’s features after module 1 are
          identical along the whole path from octahedron to trigonal prism, for any weights. At l = 3 the m = ±3 terms give a<sub>3</sub> ∝ |1 + e<sup>3iτ</sup>|² = 2(1 + cos 3τ):
          zero for the octahedron. The ligands are not blind: the distances between the two triangles change, and from module 2 on the centre reads
          their updated features.
        </p>
      </Callout>
    </section>

    <div class="wide fig-wrap">
      <Figure title="Octahedron to trigonal prism" labels={['live-calculation', 'toy-model']} number={2} id="fig-twist" framed={false}>
        <SpookyTwist client:visible />
        <Fragment slot="caption">
          Twist the upper triangle of an FeO₆ core. The metal’s s, p and d invariants (green bars) never move and the toy model’s metal features
          after one module stay identical at every τ; the f invariant and the oxygens’ features change, and the metal picks up the twist in
          module 2. The θ slider changes the d invariant but not the blindness to τ.
        </Fragment>
        <Assumptions slot="assumptions" items={twistAssumptions} />
        <Fragment slot="source">Local update and harmonics as <Cite id="unke2021spookynet" locator="Methods, Eqs. (12), (17)" />; derivation above. <code>src/lib/models/spookynet.ts</code>; the invariance along τ (to 10⁻¹²) and its loss in module 2 are tested in <code>spookynet.test.ts</code>.</Fragment>
      </Figure>
    </div>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="emb-h">
      <h2 id="emb-h" class="h-section">Charge and spin as inputs</h2>
      <p>
        The total charge and the number of unpaired electrons are embedded by an attention-like weighting over the atoms
        <Cite id="unke2021spookynet" locator="Methods, Eq. (10)" />:
      </p>
      <Math display number="5" tex={String.raw`\mathbf q_i = \mathrm{linear}(\mathbf e_{Z_i}),\qquad a_i = \frac{\Psi\,\ln\!\big(1 + e^{\mathbf q_i^{\mathsf T}\mathbf k/\sqrt F}\big)}{\sum_{j=1}^{N}\ln\!\big(1 + e^{\mathbf q_j^{\mathsf T}\mathbf k/\sqrt F}\big)},\qquad \mathbf e_{\Psi,i} = \mathrm{resmlp}(a_i\mathbf v)`} />
      <p>
        with Ψ = Q or S, learned vectors k and v (separate ones for positive and negative charge) and no biases in the resmlp, so e<sub>Ψ</sub> = 0
        when Ψ = 0 and Σ<sub>i</sub> a<sub>i</sub> = Ψ <Cite id="unke2021spookynet" locator="Methods, Eq. (10)" />. Partial charges for E<sub>ele</sub>
        are read out from f<sub>i</sub> and shifted uniformly so that they add up to Q <Cite id="unke2021spookynet" locator="Methods, Eq. (24)" />:
      </p>
      <Math display number="6" tex={String.raw`q_i = \tilde q_i + \frac{1}{N}\Big(Q - \sum_{j=1}^{N}\tilde q_j\Big),\qquad \tilde q_i = \mathbf w_q^{\mathsf T}\mathbf f_i + \tilde q_{Z_i}`} />
      <Callout kind="derivation" title="Where the total charge goes">
        <p id="embedding">
          In Eq. (5) q<sub>i</sub> is a linear function of e<sub>Z<sub>i</sub></sub> alone (the reference code passes the nuclear embedding, before
          any interaction), so each element Z has one weight w<sub>Z</sub> and a<sub>i</sub> = Ψ w<sub>Z<sub>i</sub></sub> / Σ<sub>Z</sub> n<sub>Z</sub>w<sub>Z</sub>,
          where n<sub>Z</sub> counts atoms of element Z. (i) The embedding ignores geometry: a triangular and a linear Ag₃⁺ receive the same
          e<sub>Q</sub> on every atom, and any difference between them has to come from the interaction modules. (ii) Atoms of one element share
          Ψ equally wherever they are; two identical fragments get Ψ/2 each at any separation. (iii) The correction in Eq. (6) is the same for every
          atom, so a fragment of N<sub>A</sub> atoms takes N<sub>A</sub>/N of the mismatch Q − Σq̃, whatever it is made of; AIMNet2’s neural charge
          equilibration instead shares it by learned weights (<a href="/models/aimnet2/">AIMNet2 page</a>).
        </p>
      </Callout>
      <p>
        The authors show what the embeddings are for: on GFN2-xTB data for Ag₃⁺/Ag₃⁻ and for singlet/triplet CH₂, models without them predicted
        qualitatively wrong surfaces and wrong global minima, and for CH₂ one surface for both spin states; with them the reference surfaces were
        reproduced <Cite id="unke2021spookynet" locator="Results, Electronic states; Fig. 4" />. Without a charge embedding the Ag₃ states still differed,
        because the constrained charges enter E<sub>ele</sub>, which the authors call insufficient. On QMspin carbenes the embeddings
        greatly reduced the error for spin states not seen for that structure <Cite id="unke2021spookynet" locator="Results, Electronic states" />.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="nl-h">
      <h2 id="nl-h" class="h-section">Nonlocal interactions</h2>
      <p>
        The nonlocal update is self-attention over all atoms of the structure, with queries, keys and values computed from each atom’s features
        <Cite id="unke2021spookynet" locator="Methods, Eqs. (18)–(19)" />:
      </p>
      <Math display number="7" tex={String.raw`\mathbf n_i = \frac{\sum_{j=1}^{N} \exp\!\big(\mathbf q_i^{\mathsf T}\mathbf k_j/\sqrt F\big)\,\mathbf v_j}{\sum_{j=1}^{N} \exp\!\big(\mathbf q_i^{\mathsf T}\mathbf k_j/\sqrt F\big)}`} />
      <p>
        No distance appears in Eq. (7). The motivation is physical: electrons can be delocalised, and charge or spin can move in response to distant
        changes, as in the Au₂ cluster on MgO(001) whose preferred orientation changes when Al dopants more than 10 Å away are added
        <Cite id="unke2021spookynet" locator="Introduction; Results, Nonlocal effects; Fig. 2b" /> <Cite id="ko2021fourth" />. For diatomics
        pulled apart, the authors argue that energy contributions should depend on the partner even beyond the cutoff, and show that without the
        nonlocal block a model trained on nine bond scans develops an unphysical step at large separation, which a larger cutoff only moves
        outwards <Cite id="unke2021spookynet" locator="Results, Nonlocal effects; Fig. 5" />.
      </p>
      <Callout kind="derivation" title="What separated fragments see">
        <p id="fragments">
          Let fragments A and B be farther apart than r<sub>cut</sub>, so no local sum couples them and E<sub>rep</sub> between them is zero.
          (i) Eq. (7) and Eq. (5) contain no distances, so nothing the network computes changes with the separation; only E<sub>ele</sub> and
          E<sub>vdw</sub> still do. (ii) Without the nonlocal block and with Q = S = 0, e<sub>Q</sub> = e<sub>S</sub> = 0 and every atom’s features are
          those of its fragment alone, so the network energy is E(A) + E(B). (iii) With attention, atom i in A attends to B’s keys and values,
          so its features differ from A alone and the energy keeps an offset that does not decay; whether it is small is up to training.
          (iv) If B is a copy of A, every key and value appears twice, which doubles numerator and denominator of Eq. (7) alike: identical copies
          still add up exactly, and so they do in the FAVOR+ form below. (v) With a total charge Q, two identical fragments each receive Q/2
          from Eq. (5) and from Eq. (6), so the far limit is 2E(A, Q/2), not E(A⁺) + E(A): exact theory puts a shared electron on the straight line
          between integer charges <Cite id="perdew1982fractional" locator="Abstract" />, which a model’s E(Q) need not follow (compare the AIMNet2
          page, Fig. 3).
        </p>
      </Callout>
    </section>

    <div class="wide fig-wrap">
      <Figure title="Two fragments pulled apart" labels={['live-calculation', 'toy-model']} number={3} id="fig-fragments" framed={false}>
        <SpookyFragments client:visible />
        <Fragment slot="caption">
          Pull two fragments apart with and without the nonlocal block. Beyond the cutoff (shaded) both curves are flat. A neutral pair adds up
          exactly without attention; with attention, unlike fragments keep an offset at any distance while two benzenes do not. A total charge
          splits as the embedding and Eq. (6) dictate, identically at every distance.
        </Fragment>
        <Assumptions slot="assumptions" items={fragAssumptions} />
        <Fragment slot="source">Eqs. (5)–(7) as <Cite id="unke2021spookynet" locator="Methods, Eqs. (10), (18)–(19), (24)" />; derivation above. Statements (i)–(v) are tested for the toy model in <code>src/lib/models/spookynet.test.ts</code>.</Fragment>
      </Figure>
    </div>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="favor-h">
      <h2 id="favor-h" class="h-section">Attention at linear cost</h2>
      <p>
        Eq. (7) needs all N² pairs. SpookyNet instead uses FAVOR+ <Cite id="choromanski2020performers" locator="Sec. 2" />: a random feature map
        φ with E[φ(q)·φ(k)] = exp(q·k/√F) turns the attention matrix into a product of thin matrices
        <Cite id="unke2021spookynet" locator="Methods, Eq. (20)" />,
      </p>
      <Math display number="8" tex={String.raw`\mathbf n_i \approx \frac{\phi(\mathbf q_i)^{\mathsf T}\sum_j \phi(\mathbf k_j)\,\mathbf v_j^{\mathsf T}}{\phi(\mathbf q_i)^{\mathsf T}\sum_j \phi(\mathbf k_j)},\qquad \phi(\mathbf x) = \frac{1}{\sqrt m}\exp\!\Big(\boldsymbol\omega_a^{\mathsf T}\mathbf x' - \tfrac12\|\mathbf x'\|^2\Big)_{a=1\ldots m},\quad \mathbf x' = \mathbf x/F^{1/4}`} />
      <p>
        whose cost grows linearly with N. The features are positive, which Choromanski et al. introduce because sin/cos features can give
        unstable, even negative, normalisers where kernel values are small, and the ω are drawn orthogonal to reduce the variance
        <Cite id="choromanski2020performers" locator="Sec. 2.3, Lemma 1; Sec. 2.4" />. SpookyNet uses m = F random features and draws them once at
        initialisation, so the model is deterministic <Cite id="unke2021spookynet" locator="Methods, after Eq. (20)" />; the trained network is
        then fitted with that one approximation in place, which is a fixed function, not a sample of exact attention.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="wide fig-wrap">
      <Figure title="Exact attention against FAVOR+" labels={['live-calculation', 'toy-model']} number={4} id="fig-favor" framed={false}>
        <SpookyAttention client:visible />
        <Fragment slot="caption">
          Attention weights of every atom of C₁₀H₃⁺ on every other, exact and estimated with m random features. The error shrinks roughly as
          1/√m; sharpen the attention and sin/cos features start to produce negative weights and non-positive normalisers, positive features
          never do.
        </Fragment>
        <Assumptions slot="assumptions" items={attnAssumptions} />
        <Fragment slot="source">FAVOR+: <Cite id="choromanski2020performers" locator="Sec. 2.3–2.4" />; its use in SpookyNet: <Cite id="unke2021spookynet" locator="Methods, Eq. (20)" />; random features as in OUnke/SpookyNet <code>modules/attention.py</code>. Unbiasedness, positivity and the error’s fall with m are tested in <code>spookynet.test.ts</code>.</Fragment>
      </Figure>
    </div>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="train-h">
      <h2 id="train-h" class="h-section">Training</h2>
      <p>
        The loss weighs root-mean-square errors of energies, forces and dipole moments Σ<sub>i</sub>q<sub>i</sub>r⃗<sub>i</sub>; dipoles are used to fit
        the charges because they are observables, while reference charges from a partitioning scheme can replace them when that is all a dataset
        has <Cite id="unke2021spookynet" locator="Methods, Eqs. (28)–(32)" />. Without dipoles or charges, E<sub>ele</sub> and E<sub>vdw</sub> are
        dropped. The weights are equal except for MD17 and QM7-X, where forces are weighted 100 times more; training uses AMSGrad with an
        exponential moving average of the parameters, and the paper’s models use T = 6, F = 128 and r<sub>cut</sub> = 10 a₀ unless stated
        otherwise <Cite id="unke2021spookynet" locator="Methods, Training and hyperparameters" />. Every result below comes from a model trained for
        that dataset alone.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="ev-h">
      <h2 id="ev-h" class="h-section">What the paper shows</h2>
      <p>
        On the four datasets of Ko et al. that probe nonlocal charge transfer (C₁₀H₂/C₁₀H₃⁺, Na₈/₉Cl₈⁺, Ag₃⁺/⁻ and Au₂ on doped MgO), SpookyNet
        reported lower energy, force and charge errors than the second-, third- and fourth-generation Behler–Parrinello networks; those values are
        quoted from Ko et al., not retrained, with the same data settings <Cite id="unke2021spookynet" locator="Table 1" /> <Cite id="ko2021fourth" locator="Table I" />.
        The authors add that three of the four could be modelled without nonlocal interactions as long as charge embeddings are used. On QM7-X it
        was more accurate than SchNet and PaiNN both for unseen conformations of known molecules and for entirely unseen molecules, and the QM7-X
        model optimised much larger molecules and fullerenes, which have no counterpart in its training data
        <Cite id="unke2021spookynet" locator="Table 2; Fig. 2c" />. On MD17 it matched or improved on the models compared
        <Cite id="unke2021spookynet" locator="Table 3" />; on the randomly distorted CH₄ set of Pozdnyakov et al. it kept improving with more data
        <Cite id="unke2021spookynet" locator="Supplementary Information" /> <Cite id="pozdnyakov2020completeness" />. These are published results
        from different protocols and are not combined into a ranking here; the Atlas has not rerun them.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="maths-h">
      <h2 id="maths-h" class="h-section">The mathematics at three depths</h2>
      <div class="depths">
        <input type="radio" name="depth" id="l-int" checked />
        <label for="l-int">Intuition</label>
        <input type="radio" name="depth" id="l-tech" />
        <label for="l-tech">Technical</label>
        <input type="radio" name="depth" id="l-res" />
        <label for="l-res">Research</label>
        <div class="depth depth-int">
          <p>
            Every atom is told what element it is, how charged and how magnetic the whole molecule is, and then looks around in two ways: closely,
            noting how much of its neighbourhood lies in each direction, in shapes like s, p and d orbitals; and globally, by asking every other
            atom, near or far, a question and listening most to the ones whose answers match. Physics fills in what is known exactly: nuclei repel
            at short range, charges attract and repel at long range, and dispersion pulls weakly.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="depth depth-tech">
          <p>
            Local features are invariant contractions of l ≤ 2 sums over neighbours (Eq. 3), so one module resolves up to quadrupolar angular
            structure at linear cost per neighbour; higher orders arise through the modules (Fig. 2). Q and S enter as per-atom embeddings whose
            sharing depends on composition only (Eq. 5). The nonlocal block is permutation-equivariant and distance-free (Eq. 7) and is evaluated
            with positive orthogonal random features (Eq. 8); its cost is linear in N. Charges are constrained to Σq = Q by a uniform shift
            (Eq. 6) and fitted to dipoles. Forces are −∂E/∂r by automatic differentiation, so they are conservative.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="depth depth-res">
          <p>
            The global attention makes the energy non-additive for separated unlike fragments by construction (derivation (iii) above): size
            consistency is left to the data, the price of letting distant changes matter. FAVOR+ with ω frozen at initialisation is one fixed
            positive kernel, exp-like in expectation only, so the trained network is a linear-attention model rather than an approximation to a
            softmax one. The electronic embedding is a learned, composition-only prior for where charge and spin sit; geometry-dependent
            redistribution must be learned by the modules, and the integer-to-fractional behaviour of separated fragments is not constrained
            (compare the charge equilibration of the <a href="/models/long-range/">4G-HDNNP</a> and AIMNet2’s NQE). The ground-state configuration
            features of Eq. (9) and the s/p/d interaction shapes are inductive biases that the authors link to chemically interpretable
            local chemical potentials <Cite id="unke2021spookynet" locator="Fig. 2a; Methods, local chemical potentials" />.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="out-h">
      <h2 id="out-h" class="h-section">Outputs</h2>
      <p>
        Energy and conservative forces for a given total charge and number of unpaired electrons, atomic partial charges and the dipole moment
        built from them <Cite id="unke2021spookynet" locator="Methods, Eqs. (4), (24), (26)" />. The charges are fitted to dipoles (or to a chosen
        charge partitioning) and are not an electronic-structure quantity; SpookyNet predicts no orbitals, spin densities, Fermi level or density of
        states.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="fail-h">
      <h2 id="fail-h" class="h-section">Where it can fail</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>From the architecture.</strong> A single module is blind to any change that keeps every l ≤ 2 neighbour sum, such as the trigonal
        twist of Fig. 2; deeper modules recover it only indirectly. Separated unlike fragments are coupled by attention at any distance, so a neutral
        pair need not dissociate to the sum of its parts unless training enforces it (Fig. 3). Identical fragments always split Q and S evenly, so a
        charged dimer dissociates to two half-charged fragments, at whatever energy the model gives a fractional charge. The charge embedding is
        blind to geometry and the charge correction shares by atom count. Attention over all atoms makes every prediction depend on everything in
        the structure. For periodic systems the paper names Ewald summation for E<sub>ele</sub> <Cite id="unke2021spookynet" locator="Methods, after Eq. (24)" />;
        the reference code’s attention runs over the atoms of each structure, that is, of the simulation cell and not its images. By derivation
        (iv), a supercell made of identical copies gives the same features, but what each atom attends to is whatever the chosen cell contains.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>From a trained model.</strong> There is no released checkpoint, so every trained SpookyNet is the one its user trains. The paper’s
        models were each trained on one dataset (small organic molecules, GFN2-xTB clusters and scans, the Ko et al. sets), mostly with the
        semi-empirical or DFT reference of that dataset; the authors name large heterogeneous condensed-phase systems such as solvated proteins as
        a remaining challenge <Cite id="unke2021spookynet" locator="Discussion" />. The QM7-X model’s absolute energies were systematically too
        high for large structures in a conformer benchmark, though relative conformer energies were good
        <Cite id="unke2021spookynet" locator="Results, Generalization across chemical and conformational space; Supplementary Discussion" />.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="ck-h">
      <h2 id="ck-h" class="h-section">Checkpoints</h2>
      <p>
        None. The reference implementation (MIT) ships code and the D4 reference data but no trained weights, and the paper’s models are not
        distributed. The Ag₃⁺/Ag₃⁻ and singlet/triplet carbene datasets generated for the paper are on Zenodo
        (<a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.5115732">10.5281/zenodo.5115732</a>). Note that the code’s constructor defaults (64 features,
        16 radial functions, three modules) are not the paper’s settings. The <a href="/models/checkpoints/">checkpoints page</a> lists every recorded
        checkpoint of other families.
      </p>
      <Callout kind="note" title="Related families">
        <a href="/models/aimnet2/">AIMNet2</a> also takes the total charge as input but shares it by learned per-atom weights and has no global
        attention. The <a href="/models/long-range/">4G-HDNNP</a> moves charge across a whole structure by charge equilibration, a linear solve;
        LES learns latent charges with long-range electrostatics. <a href="/models/pet/">PET</a> and <a href="/models/equiformer/">Equiformer</a> use
        attention only among neighbours inside a cutoff. <a href="/models/esen-uma/">UMA</a> takes charge and spin as inputs for molecules.
        <a href="/models/schnet/">SchNet</a> is the distance-only message passing that SpookyNet adds angles, electronic inputs and nonlocality to.
      </Callout>
    </section>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="res-h">
      <h2 id="res-h" class="h-section">Resources</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>SpookyNet: <Cite id="unke2021spookynet" />; its precursor PhysNet: <Cite id="unke2019physnet" />.</li>
        <li>FAVOR+ attention: <Cite id="choromanski2020performers" />. Dispersion: <Cite id="caldeweyher2019d4" />. Repulsion: <Cite id="ziegler1985zbl" />.</li>
        <li>Datasets with nonlocal charge transfer: <Cite id="ko2021fourth" />. Degenerate environments: <Cite id="pozdnyakov2020completeness" />.</li>
        <li>Code: <a href="https://github.com/OUnke/SpookyNet">OUnke/SpookyNet</a> (MIT, PyTorch, ASE calculator).</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </article>
